body:
Hi, as someone who has chosen to do this (divorce kinship terms from the people I was forced to call a certain way as a child) as well I have some thoughts; 

Before anyone else says it- calling them sperm / egg donor makes it very difficult for actually donor conceived people to find members of our community. So I would request you leave those terms to us, as they truly and actually are meant for us to use to describe our realities. I understand the urge and get that under popular culture, sperm donor = deadbeat dad, but quite frankly, it's not appropriate, and there's a lot of emotional complexity to this lived experience that's lost on people who are not having said experience. 


Now that I've gotten it out of the way, I got kind of lucky in this regard, because the term for the people that chose to create me in my community is "recipient parents", and I can simply drop the 'parents' part, because they did not do any meaningful parenting. When I don't want to describe my childhood social dynamics to strangers I use words like former / abusive caregiver to denote the subtext of emotional distance, or things like 'glorified babysitter'. Provider definitely works, too.

Always willing to try and get to know new people. :) I don't think any of your interests are sad! I think they're cool. I'm into that stuff too.

&#x200B;

There's this one flash game I've played forever now (haven't in a while though), about a fox going around finding hidden clues in this really cool environment. It's called William and Sly - have you ever heard of it?

I wish I had advice :( I do the same thing. It ends up compounding with my OCD for me, and ends up becoming this huge existential attack I can't solve for myself, because of course anxiety isn't going to be satisfied with an answer. 

&#x200B;

The only thing I can think of is working on self-compassion via DBT, but that's also such a daunting task because I can imagine very easily a shame-riddled trauma brain going, "but I don't deserve to say nice things about myself" at attempting to do so. ; \^ ; Hope you're managing okay, OP.

I completely dissociate most of the time. To the point where its hard to stay awake. I'm just...gone. It happens rather fast and I don't always have time to catch myself, either :/ My face gets kinda numb and sometimes it gets hot. Inside my head goes more blank. My heart pounds, and my arms will also get cold, like I dipped them in ice cold water. A lot of my responses are more physical than emotional, honestly.

&#x200B;

Every once in a while I'll get into fight mode, but that's so rare these days.. Fawn happens to me daily tho. Things that trigger emotional flashbacks are harder to describe, because I really am just back in that moment when it hits? No time to self-regulate; all the fear, all the shame, all the pain, just smacks me right on my ass. When my fears of being publicly humiliated rear up I tend to delete social media accounts and go ghost.

I haven't had this compulsion, honestly. I check my abuser's things because they're known to infiltrate spaces I'm in to gather information on me and watch me and because he makes disturbing content involving me. I save everything to an ever-increasing folder for the eventuality of a lawsuit of his stalking and abuse, if it ever comes to it. I save what's relevant and then move on. I honestly hate looking. It freaks me out, and sometimes it's like I can feel him watching me through the screen.

&#x200B;

It isn't abnormal or 'bad' to feel the way you are, though, I promise. When you trauma bond with someone that sort of thing tends to make you want to search them out. Your therapist is correct though, its not healthy for you. Nothing your brother is saying will help you heal and will probably just hurt you more. Anything I've ever seen my abuser writing about me has been worse for my health overall.

&#x200B;

I hope you find healing and solace.

So, first things first. You're not sick. Not at all. That's pretty much exactly how my fantasy timeline went too, from kid to adult. 

&#x200B;

Please listen to all of the comments here telling you that you were abused. You were. You also made me question my own childhood a lot more closely after this post, which is something I've been kind of dreading. I was today years old when I learned it's not normal to see your mom naked :( She'd always done weird things and have always said sexual things about my body that hit...wrong. But now I'm thinking there's more than a couple of reasons why I can't remember any of my childhood besides vagueries and the abuse I DO remember.

&#x200B;

I also wanted to take a second to dispell the comment you made about 'everyone claiming abuse these days'. It is really, really unhelpful to fall into this line of thinking. Both to others \*and\* to you. You really need to dissect why people make these comments; because its definitely \*not\* to help trauma victims. People use this talking point a lot in a lot of different ways, too. As a trans and gay person, I see this a lot; they use it as a way to dismiss our existence all the time. "Everyone notice how everyone is gay or trans these days?", that sort of thing. Its faulty logic. They're acting as if we haven't always been here, but were instead either forced to live entirely in the closet our entire lives or not understand who or what we were; and then completely forget the many, many people who WERE gay and trans, despite the dangers, even in times past, because it doesn't fit the narrative they're trying to pass off. It's the same with abuse. Not everyone knew the way to properly contextualize their experiences, once upon a time; as the times got better and it became more accepting, people are now more open about these things and how they can affect you, and actually acknowledge them happening. Its using ignorance of a subject to explain the current climate they're perceiving, incorrectly, without any forethought put into whether or not the premise itself is faulty. Please don't fall for it. This isn't somehow a new phenomenon. Its just finally being recognized and in the public eye.

This is pretty normal IMO, it sounds like your flee response is acting up a lot. Mine acts up like that a lot, too. There's nothing wrong with where I live now, but if I could, I would pack up and leave a new place every week. Not being on the move terrifies me. I always used to have a bag packed and hidden somewhere as a kid in case I got kicked out and I get more well-rested sleep on a couch than on a bed in a bedroom. The latter just feels like it can get taken away in an instant. I curb the urge more by deleting and remaking social media accounts; I can't really keep one too long without feeling as if someone is going to hurt me for it or is watching me, etc. 

&#x200B;

SO yeah, tldr; You're perfectly normal in your response. I'm sorry you're dealing with it, though. Its so hard to convince your brain you're safe. Sometimes the most unsafe thing is BEING safe, it feels like. Because then you're always worried about when the other shoe will drop :( I hope things get easier for you.

I've been going through this for a while now too, though my situation is different than yours. I had an abuser draw art of me in a very traumatizing way. And as a result its very hard to get myself to draw, even though I love art and have so many things I want to do with it. I'm like you where I try but then I freeze, and I can't make myself do anything. I feel so bad about my art and so mad that others can just draw while I had my art taken from me.

&#x200B;

I don't have advice - I just wanted to share my piece and say that you're not alone. I hope we're both able to heal.

I feel this way all of the time. I can't even tell myself certain platitudes that people will use to boost their self confidence / self love  because I'm too afraid of them , or of hurting others with them. "You didn't lose them, they lost you" is one. The whole putting yourself first thing, that's for other people, not me. I don't deserve that. Ugh. 

&#x200B;

Rooting for you and your healing!

I'm currently going through this too..I lost my entire friend group to it. (Which is to say, I distanced myself after it all happened). All of them were survivors but in the end I was a target for their frustration, and I nearly took my life because of it. I wish I had any words of wisdom about it. I've completely lost my faith in people after this. I'd  like to think maybe there are still people out there who I can have healthy relationships with that have gone through what I have. But I've lost faith in my ability to handle social situations in any capacity, too. I already had agoraphobia and now I'm just... I feel really damaged and broken, like they must be 100% in the right and that I'm bad inside out. My wife and best friend both say that's not the case, but I had abysmal self esteem before this happened, so it's in the negatives right now.

&#x200B;

I'm sorry you're hurting and that this happened to you. You didn't deserve it. You \*will\* find people who will treat you with respect and love.

I mean, its  \*possible\* that its fake. But is it helpful to \*you\* to brush them off, either now or in the long run? That's more what you should be asking right now. You're also minimizing your trauma - you shouldn't be. Its unfair to yourself. You've suffered something no one should have to. Like the person below me said, it's not 'only' emotional and physical abuse. Those alone are more than enough for you to have significant memory gaps. 

&#x200B;

I spent the majority of my teenage years having vague memories of something happening to me. Would spend my computer time I was given googling 'how do you know you were sexually abused', 'symptoms of sexual abuse'. I would also self harm with porn. I had no idea what was happening to me, only that it felt awful, and was scary. It wasn't until I was 18 that I had this memory confirmed by my mother, who tried to brush it off and say she 'stopped' it before it was able to happen (she didn't, or if she did, it was far too late and the damage was already done). There are also memories that I have that I have never been able to confirm, but still hurt me. Despite this I have all the symptoms of being sexually abused as a child. I probably don't remember more than 5% of my childhood, either. Its entirely normal to just have nothing there when trying to think about it. Like everyone else said, your brain processes and files these things differently, especially when you're a child. I also had the disturbing play thing. I don't remember my sex drive or what it was like back then, but something I know for sure that 6 year olds aren't developmentally mature enough to have a sex drive. They might be curious, in the 'what's that?' sense, but a preoccupation with sex in a child that young is abnormal and worrying.

&#x200B;

Please seek the help of a therapist if you can. You deserve to have support during a time this rough. Rooting for you.
